657	          <li>Pool = buddy tree over the IPPool CIDRs. State lives in <code>NodeBlock</code> objects; a restart rebuilds it.</li>
658	          <li>New node: first block = a percentage of its max pods, placed so its buddy stays free.</li>
659	          <li>Grow when free addresses fall below max(8, 20% of capacity), or when the kubelet reports "no IP addresses available". The node's own free buddy is taken first.</li>
660	          <li>Shrink: drain the emptiest block if the rest stays under 50% used; wait until empty; quarantine, then release.</li>
661	        </ul>
662	      </div>
663	      <div>
664	        <h4>Node agent (makes it true)</h4>
665	        <ul>
666	          <li>Installs a route to every other node's blocks and acknowledges them. Order: routes, then CNI config, then Active.</li>
667	          <li>Writes the <code>ptp</code> + <code>host-local</code> config; buddy blocks become one range.</li>
668	          <li>Drains blocks, reclaims leaked leases, cleans stale CNI NAT rules, reports usage from lease files.</li>
669	        </ul>
670	      </div>
671	      <div>
672	        <h4>What you are watching</h4>
673	        <ul>
674	          <li>The Deployment's pods are real: the scheduler places them, the CNI gives each a real address.</li>
675	          <li>A block stays Draining while any pod still holds an address in it. It only moves on when the last one ends.</li>
676	          <li>The log lines tagged <b>agent</b> are the node agents' own logs; <b>controller</b> lines are its Kubernetes events.</li>
677	        </ul>
678	      </div>
679	    </div>
680	  );
681	}
